<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
	Who may see the conversations of the agents and what they kept about an account (Ticket #30144):
	the owner, plus administrators.

	Model access is deny-by-default, and TL-Script hides every object the acting user may not see
	(R4 of the Discover specification). Without this file, a user who is no administrator finds none
	of their own chats - not through referers() on a subject, not through all(), and not as the
	result of a script function such as chatStart() - in an application with model-based security.

	Built the way tl-ai-core's binary policy is (tl-ai-core.binarySecurity.config.xml), as a policy
	of its own, since it grants more than reading:

	  1. The technical role "AiChatOwner", held by nobody globally, means "this conversation, or
	     this memory, is yours".
	  2. Role rules of type "reference" derive it per object from the object's own "owner". A
	     session without an owner - one of a background job or a schedule - has no holder, so only
	     administrators and code in a system context see it.
	  3. The security configuration grants the role Read and Write on a session, and Read on a
	     memory. Write is what lets the owner edit a session's title in a form. Creating and
	     deleting sessions and memories goes through the AgentExecutionStore, which writes in a
	     system context and checks the ownership itself (aiSessionDelete, aiMemoryDelete), so no
	     Create or Delete grant is written here.

	The parts of a conversation - its runs, the questions a run asks, the plan of the conversation -
	have no rights of their own: each delegates the whole decision to the object it belongs to, a
	run and a plan item to their session, a question to its run. Whoever may read a session may read
	its parts, and nobody else.

	A memory decides through its own owner, not through its session: the session reference is
	optional, and a memory outlives the conversation it came out of.

	Administrators and code in a system context bypass the check above all of this
	(SecurityConfigurationService.isAllowedBypass); nothing here grants them anything.

	See the developer article ai/security/administration (the shipped access policy for
	conversations).
-->
<application xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0">
	<services>
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialRolesManager">
			<instance>
				<roles>
					<!-- Described by "role.description.AiChatOwner" in this module's application bundle. -->
					<role name="AiChatOwner"/>
				</roles>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			Contributed additively to whatever access manager the application runs; no "class" is
			named, for the reason tl-ai-core.binarySecurity.config.xml gives.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.manager.AccessManager">
			<instance>
				<role-rules>
					<rules>
						<rule id="tl.ai.execution.session.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:AgentSession"
							role="AiChatOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:AgentSession#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="tl.ai.execution.memory.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:UserMemory"
							role="AiChatOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:UserMemory#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
					</rules>
				</role-rules>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.model.security.SecurityConfigurationService">
			<instance>
				<security-config>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:AgentSession">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiChatOwner"
						/>
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Write"
							roles="AiChatOwner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:UserMemory">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiChatOwner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:AgentExecution">
						<access-parent>
							<target reference="tl.ai.execution:AgentExecution#session"/>
						</access-parent>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:AgentTask">
						<access-parent>
							<target reference="tl.ai.execution:AgentTask#session"/>
						</access-parent>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:InteractionRequest">
						<access-parent>
							<target reference="tl.ai.execution:InteractionRequest#execution"/>
						</access-parent>
					</class>
				</security-config>
			</instance>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
